===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move [[downwards]], to [[fall]], to [[drop]].
#: {{ux|en|The dollar has '''declined''' rapidly since 2001.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[weak]]er or [[worse]].
#: {{ux|en|My health '''declined''' in winter.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bend downward; to bring down; to depress; to cause to bend, or fall.
#* {{RQ:Thomson Spring|year=1768|passage=in melancholy site, with head '''declined'''}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=IX|stanza=10|passage=And now faire Phoebus gan '''decline''' in hast / His weary wagon to the Westerne vale.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to decrease or diminish.
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|The Honest Man's Fortune|2|2|passage=You have '''declin'd''' his means.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd
|passage=He knoweth his error, but will not seek to '''decline''' it.}}
# To turn or bend aside; to deviate; to stray; to withdraw.
#: ''a line that '''declines''' from straightness''
#: ''conduct that '''declines''' from sound morals''
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|99|157|passage=Yet do I not '''decline''' from thy testimonies.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To choose not to do something; [[refuse]], [[forbear]], [[refrain]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1626|author=w:Philip Massinger|title=w:The Roman Actor
|passage=Could I '''decline''' this dreadful hour?}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=7|passage=“{{...}} This is Mr. Churchill, who, as you are aware, is good enough to come to us for his diaconate, and, as we hope, for much longer; and being a gentleman of independent means, he '''declines''' to take any payment.” Saying this Walden rubbed his hands together and smiled contentedly.}}
#: {{ux|en|On reflection I think I will '''decline''' your generous offer.}}
# {{senseid|en|grammar, apply inflection}} {{lb|en|transitive|grammar|usually of substantives, adjectives and pronouns}} To [[inflect]] for [[case]], [[number]], [[gender]], and the like.
#: {{syn|en|declense<qq:rare><!--and cringey-->}}
#: {{hypo|en|misdecline}}
# {{senseid|en|grammar, recite declension}} {{lb|en|transitive|grammar}} To [[recite]] all the different declined forms of (a word): to recite its [[declension]].
#: {{syn|en|declense<qq:rare><!--and cringey-->}}
#: {{hypo|en|misdecline}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1570|author=w:Roger Ascham|title=The Scholemaster|edition=1st
|passage=after the first '''declining''' of a noun and a verb<!--probably not the original text, as it looks too modern. Meh. -->}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To run through from first to last; to recite in order as though declining a noun.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|4|4|passage='''Decline''' all this, and see what now thou art. For happy wife, a most distressed widow; <br>For joyful mother, one that wails the name; <br>For one being sued to, one that humbly sues; <br>For queen , a very caitiff crown'd with care{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|American football|Canadian football}} To reject a penalty against the opposing team, usually because the result of accepting it would benefit the non-penalized team less than the preceding play.
#: ''The team chose to '''decline''' the fifteen-yard penalty because their receiver had caught the ball for a thirty-yard gain.''

====Usage notes====
* ''Decline'', ''refuse'', ''forbear'', ''refrain'': ''Decline'' is gentler than ''[[refuse]]'' and carries a connotation that the non-acceptance is an acceptable or anticipated option (''decline an invitation'') or the result of a considered decision (''the judge declined to grant the motion''). ''Refuse'' has a stronger connotation of rejection, firmness, resistance, or non-compliance. For example, if someone ''declines'' to give their name, that suggests they were given a choice and elected not to give their name. If someone ''refuses'' to give their name, the connotation is more toward a suggestion that they normally should have given their name and are being intransigent. ''[[forbear|Forbear]]'' or ''[[refrain]]'', conversely, suggest choosing not to do something that one might indulge in or be tempted to do (''refrain from smoking''), with ''forbear'' having an added connotation of showing some fortitude in withstanding the temptation (''forbear to show anger''). ''Refrain'' can also be used to refer to a general policy or preference rather than a choice on a single occasion.
* The noun form of most meanings of ''decline'' is ''decline'': ''the decline of the empire''. The noun for ''decline'' in the grammatical senses is [[declension]]. The noun [[declination]] has to do with astronomical coordinates, and is rare or archaic as the noun form of the verb ''decline''.

